FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:411 — The production child-process timeout/kill branch (`Err(RecvTimeoutError::Timeout)` → `child.kill()`/`wait()`) has no test on either side. The only "hung workload" test, tests/bench_test.rs:243-248, calls `bench::run_with_budget`, which is `#[cfg(test)]` (tests/bench/mod.rs:240) and runs the closure in an in-process thread; it never reaches `measure_in_child`. So the claim in REPORT.md:104 that "production measurements run in a child process that is killed at the deadline" is unverified by the gate. — Fix: add a hidden worker workload (or a `--worker` hook) that sleeps, then a test that drives `run_workload` with a tiny budget and asserts `BudgetExceeded` and that no child survives. — VERIFIED: traced `edge_a_hung_workload_fails_loudly_at_its_budget` → `run_with_budget` at mod.rs:241-268; `measure_in_child` is only entered via `measure_in_worker` (mod.rs:318-337), which no test forces through a timeout.

2. [MAJOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:495-547 — The new worker protocol parser has many failure branches (`ERR` prefix, wrong field count, unreadable nanos, unreadable hex, unknown tag, non-UTF-8 text) with no tests. `parse_worker_output` is not `#[cfg(test)]`, but every integration test only exercises the happy `OK …` line emitted by a known workload. A regression in any branch silently becomes `BenchError::Load` or a mis-decoded answer. — Fix: unit-test `parse_worker_output` directly with each malformed/`ERR` line and a non-UTF-8 hex payload. — VERIFIED: read tests/bench_test.rs in full; no test calls `parse_worker_output` or supplies malformed worker output.

3. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:403-405 — On the `Ok(Err(error))` read-error branch `child.wait()` is called without `child.kill()`. If `read_to_string` fails while the child is still alive, `wait()` blocks indefinitely and the budget is bypassed. The fixed workloads emit ASCII, so this is hard to reach today, but it contradicts the "budget bounds the wait" promise. — Fix: `child.kill()` before `child.wait()` on every error branch.

4. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:23-31 — The `DEFAULT_BUDGET` doc comment still says the runner "enforces this itself with a worker thread and `recv_timeout`" and that "a timed-out worker is detached and may keep running", which is the pre-refactor behaviour. The code now kills a child at mod.rs:411-414 and REPORT.md:104 says so. — Fix: update the comment to match the child-process design.

5. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:929-937 — `parse_baseline_markdown` silently skips any four-cell row whose first cell is unknown when the size cell is non-numeric, and `comparison_table` (mod.rs:1041-1046) only requires a non-empty baseline. A baseline missing a workload (or a row with a typo'd name and malformed size) therefore compares "as if it had fewer workloads", contradicting the doc at mod.rs:902-909. — Fix: after parsing, require exactly the four known workload names, or reject every four-cell row that does not name a known workload.

6. [MAJOR] phases/phase-073/REPORT.md:87 — The gate count is internally inconsistent and appears not to come from a run. If the five `rb-bench` bin unit tests are in the preserved `HEAD` (as the sentence says), they were already counted in the reported 2699, so `2699 + 4 = 2703`, not 2708; if they were added by this run, they would account for ~86 lines of `tests/bench/mod.rs` that the report attributes only to the child-process move. Line 88 also reports verify counted "4 rust / 4 edge / 4 failure-asserting" while the four new tests include only one that asserts an error. — Fix: paste the actual `cargo test --all-targets` and `verify.sh` output, or correct the decomposition. — VERIFIED: cannot run tests read-only; this would be settled by `cargo test --all-targets -- --list | grep -c ': test$'` on `HEAD` and on the working tree.

